The function's length property is essential to many techniques, such as currying functions.

comments.body:
This kind of reflection is generally not supported by the compiler as evident by its restrictions imposed on eval:  

http://code.google.com/closure/compiler/docs/limitations.html

In the general case, referring to length on anything other than named function expression is not safe:

function f(a,b,c) {return f.length};
console.info( f() );
var g = f;
f = function(a) {};
console.info( g() );

For a named function expression the could replace the length with a constant even in simple mode (although it doesn't currently):

var f = function me(a,b,c){ return me.length() };

could then be transformed to (after unused variable removal):

var f = function() {return 3};

Pardon my initial post. I accidentally doubled the code. Should have been:

function foo (bar, baz) {
  return bar;
}
alert (foo.length);


I don't think this changes your response to the issue though, but who knows. I fail to see how restrictions on eval is relevant to this issue, given one is working with SIMPLE_OPTIMIZATIONS. Given the description for ADVANCED_OPTIMIZATIONS, I can understand removal of (usually) extraneous function parameters.

If this issue does not get solved (via directly fixing the issue or allowing annotations for it or whatnot), it should be documented in the SIMPLE_OPTIMIZATIONS section on your limitations page. As it stands, the SIMPLE_OPTIMIZATIONS section gives no clue that it may remove parameters.

For my purpose, it is enough to remove the following lines

-    for (Scope fnScope : allFunctionScopes) {
-      removeUnreferencedFunctionArgs(fnScope);
-    }

from src/com/google/javascript/jscomp/RemoveUnusedVars.java .

-- Cheers, Keigo

