comments.body:
This isn't quite right.
1) x isn't always consider "MyClass|null", while assignments are restricted to subtypes of MyClass or null, the actual value type in the flow are known.

2) While it is true that in general we don't want to warn about potential null values (there are too many false positives), we can and do warn about values that can only be null:

/** @type {Object} */
var x;
x = null;
x.call();

produces:

JSC_TYPE_MISMATCH: No properties on this expression
found   : null
required: Object at line 7 character 0
x.call();

As has been brought up before, initial type of x in the example is currently wrong, it should be declared to be "MyClass|null" but its initial value at declaration should be "null" in the flow.

I think the reason we did it this way is because there's a more general problem. For any expression

/** @type {T} */
var x = getT();

There are values of T where it makes sense to trust the type annotation, and there are values of T where it makes sense to trust the return type of getT. A classic example is where T is Array.<Element> and getT is Array.<?>

I can probably suggest a fix for this that will get most of the obvious cases, but I don't know how many new warnings it will raise.

