Using the "safe" version of hasOwnProperties will work:

Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(foo, 'a');

and protects against a property called "hasOwnProperty"

In the Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty case, foo is passed as an argument and the compiler doesn't know if the callee will use foo's properties, so it doesn't remove them. For example, the same happens if you write

